  "evidence": [
    {
      "atomId": "atom-1",
      "spans": [
        {
          "id": "doc-cd904599bef7::sentence-2",
          "documentId": "doc-cd904599bef7",
          "text": "In 1989, Sir Tim Berners-Lee invented the World Wide Web (see the original proposal). ",
          "start": 8,
          "end": 94,
          "contextSpans": [
            {
              "id": "doc-cd904599bef7::sentence-3",
              "documentId": "doc-cd904599bef7",
              "text": "He coined the term \"World Wide Web,\" wrote the first World Wide Web server, \"httpd,\" and the first client program (a browser and editor), \"WorldWideWeb,\" in October 1990.\n",
              "start": 94,
              "end": 265,
              "direction": "NEXT"
            }
          ],
          "context": "In 1989, Sir Tim Berners-Lee invented the World Wide Web (see the original proposal). He coined the term \"World Wide Web,\" wrote the first World Wide Web server, \"httpd,\" and the first client program (a browser and editor), \"WorldWideWeb,\" in October 1990."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-5e89b4a67d9f::sentence-4",
          "documentId": "doc-5e89b4a67d9f",
          "text": "In November 1990, this “web of information nodes in which the user can browse at will” was formalised as a proposal, “WorldWideWeb: Proposal for a HyperText Project”, by Berners-Lee, together with a CERN colleague, Robert Cailliau. ",
          "start": 421,
          "end": 653,
          "contextSpans": [
            {
              "id": "doc-5e89b4a67d9f::sentence-3",
              "documentId": "doc-5e89b4a67d9f",
              "text": "Sir Tim Berners-Lee’s first proposal in March 1989 was for an internet-based hypertext system to link and access information across different computers. ",
              "start": 268,
              "end": 421,
              "direction": "PREVIOUS"
            }
          ],
          "context": "Sir Tim Berners-Lee’s first proposal in March 1989 was for an internet-based hypertext system to link and access information across different computers. In November 1990, this “web of information nodes in which the user can browse at will” was formalised as a proposal, “WorldWideWeb: Proposal for a HyperText Project”, by Berners-Lee, together with a CERN colleague, Robert Cailliau."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-cd904599bef7::sentence-3",
          "documentId": "doc-cd904599bef7",
          "text": "He coined the term \"World Wide Web,\" wrote the first World Wide Web server, \"httpd,\" and the first client program (a browser and editor), \"WorldWideWeb,\" in October 1990.\n",
          "start": 94,
          "end": 265,
          "contextSpans": [
            {
              "id": "doc-cd904599bef7::sentence-2",
              "documentId": "doc-cd904599bef7",
              "text": "In 1989, Sir Tim Berners-Lee invented the World Wide Web (see the original proposal). ",
              "start": 8,
              "end": 94,
              "direction": "PREVIOUS"
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-cd904599bef7::sentence-4",
              "documentId": "doc-cd904599bef7",
              "text": "He wrote the first version of the \"HyperText Markup Language\" (HTML), the document formatting language with the capability for hypertext links that became the primary publishing format for the Web. ",
              "start": 265,
              "end": 463,
              "direction": "NEXT"
            }
          ],
          "context": "In 1989, Sir Tim Berners-Lee invented the World Wide Web (see the original proposal). He coined the term \"World Wide Web,\" wrote the first World Wide Web server, \"httpd,\" and the first client program (a browser and editor), \"WorldWideWeb,\" in October 1990. He wrote the first version of the \"HyperText Markup Language\" (HTML), the document formatting language with the capability for hypertext links that became the primary publishing format for the Web."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-5e89b4a67d9f::sentence-2",
          "documentId": "doc-5e89b4a67d9f",
          "text": "This proposal went on to become the World Wide Web (WWW), whose impact on society has been profound.\n",
          "start": 167,
          "end": 268,
          "contextSpans": [
            {
              "id": "doc-5e89b4a67d9f::sentence-1",
              "documentId": "doc-5e89b4a67d9f",
              "text": "Thirty-five years ago, a young computer expert working at CERN wrote a proposal that combined accessing information with a desire for broad connectivity and openness. ",
              "start": 0,
              "end": 167,
              "direction": "PREVIOUS"
            }
          ],
          "context": "Thirty-five years ago, a young computer expert working at CERN wrote a proposal that combined accessing information with a desire for broad connectivity and openness. This proposal went on to become the World Wide Web (WWW), whose impact on society has been profound."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-5e89b4a67d9f::sentence-3",
          "documentId": "doc-5e89b4a67d9f",
          "text": "Sir Tim Berners-Lee’s first proposal in March 1989 was for an internet-based hypertext system to link and access information across different computers. ",
